window.docContents[210].resolve({"/Parabolic-Equations/Diffusion-driven-linear-instability/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-driven-linear-instability":{"contents":"Diffusion damps each scalar Fourier mode of the heat equation. In a coupled\nsystem, unequal diffusion rates can instead destabilize a reaction matrix\nwhose spatially homogeneous evolution is stable. For\n\n\n\na cosine of frequency  evolves by the matrix .\nThe theorem finds a growing mode from the raw coefficient inequalities.\n\nSuppose  and, writing ,Then the homogeneous ODE is asymptotically stable, but the linear PDE on\nthe line admits arbitrarily small uniformly bounded initial perturbations\nwhose first component at the origin grows without bound in magnitude. The\nconstructed solutions are cosine modes  with\n. There is also an explicitly chosen interval\n on which these solutions have zero Neumann derivatives at both\nendpoints and spatially nonconstant initial data.The interval length is determined by the unstable frequency, and the\ninstability is that of the linear system.\n\nThe trace and determinant of  give homogeneous asymptotic stability.\nFor a spatial mode with , the determinant becomes\n. The strict inequalities make this quadratic\nnegative for some , so the mode matrix has a positive real\neigenvalue  and a nonzero eigenvector with nonzero first\ncomponent. Multiplying that eigenvector by a sufficiently small scalar\ngives any requested initial uniform bound, while \nforces growth at the origin. Setting the interval length to \nmakes the sine factors in both boundary derivatives vanish.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations","header":"6.6. Diffusion-driven linear instability","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Diffusion-driven-linear-instability/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-driven-linear-instability"}});